NATIVE PICTURE CODEC VERIFICATION

2026-11-17. All 61 initial Linux CLI/container checks passed: one-pixel and
255/256-run boundaries, independent headers/checksums, 1080p, 3840x2160,
worst-case alternating 4K pixels, exact RGB/P6 round trips, malformed headers,
truncation/trailing bytes, record errors, bounded dimensions, P6 comments/CRLF,
existing-file preservation and injected write/fsync failures.

All six image fixtures produced byte-identical ZPIX and decoded RGB on native
Linux and Windows/Wine. Ten malformed Windows inputs were rejected and an
existing destination was preserved. The final bounded shared decoder also
rejected five upper-word corruptions; six decoded image arrays matched prior
results. Windows final decoder repeated the 1080p round trip and upper-word
overflow rejection successfully.

Independent original Zero regression media/picture-check.zero passed 42/42
on Linux and Windows/Wine, with full raster checks, input preservation and
output guard bytes. A separate reviewer also checked exact-path/hardlink/
symlink output aliases and checksum failures with new/existing destinations.
No remaining defect was found in that review.

These are codec and native-tool checks. A .zpix desktop viewer and an OS
render command are not part of this change. Native Windows tests used Wine;
they do not establish physical Windows or a specific desktop GUI integration.

